  <h1>Java Data Types</h1>

  <p>Data types specify the different sizes and values that can be stored in a variable.</p>
  <p>Java is a strongly typed language, which means every variable must be declared with a data type.</p>

  <p><strong>Common Java Data Types:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>int</strong> - for integers (e.g., 1, 100, -50)</li>
    <li><strong>float</strong> - for floating-point numbers (e.g., 3.14, -0.99)</li>
    <li><strong>char</strong> - for single characters (e.g., 'A', 'z')</li>
    <li><strong>boolean</strong> - for true/false values</li>
    <li><strong>String</strong> - for text values (e.g., "Hello")</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Simple Java Code:</strong></p>
  <pre><code>
public class DataTypes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int myNum = 25;
        float myFloat = 3.14f;
        char myChar = 'A';
        boolean isJavaFun = true;
        String message = "Java is powerful!";
        
        System.out.println(myNum);
        System.out.println(myFloat);
        System.out.println(myChar);
        System.out.println(isJavaFun);
        System.out.println(message);
    }
}
  </code></pre>

